WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.694
Size each worker for the models it will serve.

00:00:02.694 --> 00:00:05.456
GPU architecture determines supported acceleration features,

00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:09.814
while VRAM determines whether model weights, context cache, and runtime overhead fit.

00:00:09.814 --> 00:00:12.612
More GPUs do not create shared memory automatically.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:16.704
A runtime must support tensor or pipeline parallelism to divide one model across devices,

00:00:16.704 --> 00:00:19.896
and cross-device communication may become the bottleneck.

00:00:19.896 --> 00:00:23.464
Replicating a model on separate GPUs is often simpler when the goal is concurrent

00:00:23.464 --> 00:00:23.991
capacity.

00:00:23.991 --> 00:00:27.272
CPUs handle tokenization, request processing, data movement,

00:00:27.272 --> 00:00:28.856
and services that surround inference.

00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:31.359
System RAM must accommodate model loading, caches,

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:33.473
and non-GPU components without swapping.

00:00:33.473 --> 00:00:37.001
Use fast local SSD storage for active model copies when possible;

00:00:37.001 --> 00:00:41.098
repeatedly loading large models over a shared network can delay startup and turn storage

00:00:41.098 --> 00:00:42.747
into a cluster-wide dependency.

00:00:42.747 --> 00:00:44.810
Network design follows traffic patterns.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:49.282
Small text prompts may use little bandwidth, but model distribution, multimodal inputs,

00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:52.720
logging, backups, and distributed inference can move substantial data.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.349
Measure both bandwidth and latency.

00:00:54.349 --> 00:00:58.191
Choose switches with enough nonblocking capacity, appropriate port speeds,

00:00:58.191 --> 00:01:00.965
and management features for segmentation and monitoring.

00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:04.507
A fast link cannot compensate for an oversubscribed switch, poor cabling,

00:01:04.507 --> 00:01:08.185
or a design that transfers model state unnecessarily between nodes.

00:01:08.185 --> 00:01:09.844
Account for physical infrastructure.

00:01:09.844 --> 00:01:14.270
Confirm power circuits, outlet distribution, power-supply headroom, heat removal, airflow,

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.353
and noise before installing dense compute.

00:01:16.353 --> 00:01:19.248
Rack systems improve serviceability and cable management,

00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:22.106
while desktop systems can be economical for a small lab.

00:01:22.106 --> 00:01:25.888
Consumer hardware may offer strong performance per dollar but usually has fewer

00:01:25.888 --> 00:01:26.892
remote-management,

00:01:26.892 --> 00:01:29.326
redundancy, validation, and support features.

00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:34.390
Document those tradeoffs rather than assuming low purchase cost means low operational

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:34.400
cost.
